WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Let's read this short program together to learn something about the concept

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.920
scope. We talked about scope a little bit in the variables lessons. Alright, let's

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.920
read the program. On line 2, it says var points get 0, which means that we have

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.160
created the variable points and assigned it the value 0. Then we call the

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.200
function changePoints, which we can read down here. We create the variable boom

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.760
and assign it 5, and then we do something kind of weird, which you might remember:

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.600
we create the variable points again and assign it 1,000. We're gonna pay close

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.840
attention to this line of code in a little bit. Then the function says, or

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.920
console logs, "Inside the function I have" the value of points, plus "points." The

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.960
function is over, so we go back to line 5, and it says, "Outside the function I have

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:59.240
points points," so whatever the value of points is. We would expect this to be the

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:09.440
same in both places, but let's run it and see what happens. Alright, that was kind

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.520
of fast, but we can see down here in the console what went on. It says, "Inside the

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.040
function I have 1,000 points." That makes sense because we assigned the value

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:24.080
1,000 right before it on line 11, on line 10, and then on line 11, it said, "I have

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.160
1,000 points." But up here, it says, "Outside the function I have 0 points,"

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.720
and that makes no sense because we didn't change the value in between here

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.920
on line 11 and on line 5. Once again, the difference is that variables that are

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.240
created inside of a function are only available inside of that function, and we

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.640
can see that with this variable boom. At the end of the program, the watch window

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.240
is telling us that boom is not available. That's because it was created here

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.400
inside of the function, and so now that the next line of code has moved

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:07.840
on, the program just doesn't know anything about boom. Meanwhile, we created

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.060
the variable points twice, but because this time we did it inside of a function,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.400
the program thinks that you want a new variable that is only accessible here. In

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.780
fact, your program has two variables called points, which is very confusing,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:34.440
and you probably don't want to do it. The best way to change this is to just delete

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:47.520
that var command. If we run it again, you'll see that now we're not trying to

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.840
create the variable on line 10. The program understands that we're talking

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.480
about the same variable in both places, and so in each case, it says you have

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.200
1,000 points. Once again, boom is not available, which makes sense because we

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.760
still are creating it here inside the function. Variables that are created

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.680
outside of any function are said to have global scope, and scope just means where

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.160
in the program can you talk about or use this variable. So points, you can use

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.360
anywhere. Boom has local scope, which means that it can only be used inside of

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.160
the function or onEvent where it was created. As you're debugging programs, pay

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.840
close attention to where you're using the var command. There are definitely

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.880
reasons you would want to create a variable with local scope like boom, and

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:42.920
we'll talk about these more in this class, but for now, keep making variables

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:50.040
once at the top of your program and outside of any functions or onEvents.
